---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../components/Quiz.astro';

const questions = [
  {
    question: "What happens to a message in a traditional queue once a consumer reads and acknowledges it?",
    options: [
      "It stays in the queue for other consumers",
      "It gets deleted from disk or memory immediately",
      "It gets moved to a separate log for auditing",
      "It gets replicated to every other consumer automatically"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "A traditional queue works on a process-and-destroy model. Once one consumer acknowledges a message, the broker removes it, and nobody else can read it."
  },
  {
    question: "What happens to an event in a log-based system like Kafka once a consumer reads it?",
    options: [
      "It is deleted the moment that consumer finishes",
      "It is not deleted, only the consumer's offset (its bookmark) moves forward",
      "It is compressed and archived separately",
      "It is converted into a message and requeued"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "Kafka's log is append-only and immutable. Reading an event never removes it; only the reading consumer's offset advances."
  },
  {
    question: "Why can an Elasticsearch pipeline and a Spark job both read the exact same Kafka topic from the beginning of time without interfering with each other?",
    options: [
      "Because Kafka duplicates the topic for every new consumer",
      "Because each consumer tracks its own offset independently, and reading does not remove events from the log",
      "Because Kafka only allows one consumer per topic",
      "Because both jobs share the same offset"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "Since the log is never destroyed and each consumer keeps its own bookmark, any number of independent readers can replay the same history without stepping on each other."
  },
  {
    question: "What does Kleppmann say a Kafka-style log has in common with a database's write-ahead log (WAL)?",
    options: [
      "Both are deleted as soon as a transaction commits",
      "Both are immutable, append-only, sequential records that let readers rebuild state by replaying from a remembered point",
      "Both require the consumer to acknowledge and destroy each entry",
      "Neither one is ever written to disk"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "A WAL and a streaming log share the same architecture: an append-only record that a reader can replay sequentially to reconstruct state, whether that reader is a database replica or a stream consumer."
  },
  {
    question: "What is the shared principle behind a WAL and a streaming log, according to this pill?",
    options: [
      "Speed over durability, both prioritize being fast over being complete",
      "Durability over volatility: disk is the source of truth, and any downstream state can be rebuilt from it after a failure",
      "Volatility over durability, both are meant to be temporary buffers",
      "Neither concept applies to real production databases"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "Both treat structured disk as the permanent source of truth, unlike a traditional queue, which treats storage as a temporary buffer meant to be emptied quickly."
  },
  {
    question: "Why was Kafka designed around disk persistence instead of pure in-memory speed?",
    options: [
      "Disk was the only storage available at the time",
      "Because the log itself is meant to be the system of record, and every consumer, materialized view, or derived table is a projection built by reading it",
      "Because in-memory storage cannot be partitioned",
      "Because consumers cannot track offsets in memory"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "Kafka treats the log as the durable source of truth. Everything downstream, consumers, materialized views, derived tables, is rebuilt by reading it, which only works if the log itself persists."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Quiz: Topics vs Queues | Streaming Pill 3"
  description="Test your understanding of why a Kafka topic behaves nothing like a traditional queue, and how it mirrors a database's write-ahead log."
>
  <section class="pill-quiz-page">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/blog/streaming-pill-3-topics-are-not-queues" class="back-link">← Back to Pill 3</a>

      <div class="pill-header">
        <div class="badge badge-series" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Pill 3 · Topics vs Queues</div>
        <h1>Quiz: Does Reading Delete It?</h1>
        <p class="pill-lead">
          These questions test whether you understand what actually separates a topic from a
          queue, and why that difference mirrors a database's write-ahead log.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Quiz
        title="Pill 3: A Topic Is Not a Queue"
        questions={questions}
      />

      <div class="pill-footer glass-card">
        <h3>How did it go?</h3>
        <p>
          If you scored below 70%, re-read <strong>Pill 3</strong>, and picture two different
          systems reading from the same topic. Does one of them reading affect the other?
        </p>
        <div class="pill-footer-links">
          <a href="/blog/streaming-pill-3-topics-are-not-queues" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm">Re-read Pill 3</a>
          <a href="/pills/streaming-quiz-4" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">Next: Pill 4 Quiz — Micro-Batching</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .pill-quiz-page {
    padding: 3.5rem 0 5rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.8rem;
    transition: color 0.2s ease;
  }
  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--accent-blue);
  }

  .badge-series {
    background: var(--accent-blue);
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  .pill-header {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .pill-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.5vw, 2.2rem);
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .pill-lead {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 640px;
  }

  .pill-footer {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    background: var(--bg-card);
  }

  .pill-footer h3 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--text-main);
  }

  .pill-footer p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
    max-width: 600px;
  }

  .pill-footer-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.65rem;
  }
</style>
